################Playbooks####################

Playbooks are the scripts that the Ansible binary will consume to perform Automation. 

Review a Playbook

- YAML
	- Helps with readability
	- Indentation very important
	- spaces, not tabs
		- Playbooks fail with tabs
	- Indentation is how YAML shows precedence
	- Good practice is two spaces when Indenting

######### Playbook components #################

- Play
	- You are the coach; playbook tells player what to do
	- Can have multiple plays
	- Play defines new set of hosts
	- Name optional, but should be used
		- Acts as documentation, shows up in output
	- Hosts: comma-seperated list of hosts and/or groups
		---
		- name: Perform package updates
		  hosts: web
		  gather_facts: false

- Task
	- Ordered list of automations
	- Performed in sequence
	- Complete first task for all host
	- Each task accomplishes one thing
		task:
		 - name: Update all packages on system
		   ansible.builtin.dnf:
			name: "*"
			state: latest
- Modules
	- Refernce Python or Powershell
	- No programming knowledge needed
	- Fraction of existing modules included in Community edtion
	- Addtional modules delivered as collections
		ansible.builtin.dnf:
			name: "*"
			state: latest

- Collection
	- Collection: archive in TAR format
	- Places files on Ansible server
	- Fully qualified collection name (FQCN): using full
		naming when calling module in playbooks.


################### Inventory #######################

- Inventory
	- Big list of all available hosts
	- Playbooks hosts reference things in Inventory
	- Inventory: menu
	- Playbook hosts: order
	- When executing a playbook, an inventory must be specified

* Inventory are stored in INI or YAML format.
	INI older/most common

- INI Inventory Details
	- Fully qualified domain name(FQDN): DNS Resolvable.
	- FQDN: done
	- Magic variable: known to ansible
	- ansible_host: use this IP to connect
	- Custom  variable: arbitrary name
* Learn YAM vs INI

- Dynamic Inventory Source
	- Run a script to build Inventory
	- Configuration management database(CMDB): remote
		source storing host information
	- Examples:
		- ServiceNow
		- CSV
	- Keeps inventories from going stale

